      <a class="back" href="/">Back to homepage</a>
      <p class="eyebrow">Network Troubleshooting</p>
      <h1>{title}</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        BGP route flapping is rarely a single-command problem. In production, the hard part is not only finding the unstable prefix or peer, but also understanding the blast radius before making a change.
      </p>

      <h2>Start With Symptoms, Not Assumptions</h2>
      <p>
        I first confirm what is actually unstable: a neighbor session, a set of prefixes, a route-map result, or an upstream path. A BGP session can remain established while individual routes flap because of policy changes, next-hop reachability, IGP instability, or external provider behavior.
      </p>
      <p>
        The initial evidence usually comes from monitoring alerts, route table snapshots, syslog, interface counters, and user-facing symptoms. I avoid jumping directly into configuration changes until I can describe the failure in one sentence.
      </p>

      <h2>Check the Control Plane</h2>
      <p>
        The first technical checkpoint is the BGP neighbor state. I check uptime, reset reason, keepalive and hold timers, received prefix count, rejected prefix count, and whether the peer is hitting route limits. If the neighbor itself is bouncing, I move down the stack to interface errors, link stability, MTU, CPU pressure, and transport reachability.
      </p>

      <h2>Verify Next-Hop Reachability</h2>
      <p>
        Many route flapping incidents are not caused by BGP itself. BGP may only be reporting instability from the underlying network. If a next hop depends on OSPF, static routes, MPLS, or a tunnel interface, I validate that dependency. A flapping IGP route can make a BGP path appear unstable even when the BGP peer is healthy.
      </p>

      <h2>Review Policy Changes</h2>
      <p>
        Route-maps, prefix lists, AS-path filters, communities, and local preference changes can create unintended behavior. I compare recent changes against the affected prefixes and peers. In larger environments, a policy update on one edge can shift traffic unexpectedly and expose asymmetric routing or capacity limits elsewhere.
      </p>

      <h2>Contain Before Correcting</h2>
      <p>
        In production, the safest fix is often staged. I may temporarily dampen a noisy prefix, prefer a stable backup path, adjust local preference, or coordinate with the provider before applying a permanent configuration change. The goal is to reduce impact first, then remove the root cause.
      </p>

      <h2>Post-Incident Notes</h2>
      <p>
        After service stabilizes, I document the affected prefixes, peer, timeline, trigger, verification commands, and final action. Good post-incident notes turn a stressful routing event into a reusable operational playbook.
      </p>
    </main>
